{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2654 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "6726d7e1f6c1e67cbbe230a074c92210c566467328a23f465e93a2f859370e89" :edb/db-before-t 2653 :edb/db-after-t 2654 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535966 50 "2026-10-15T13:05:24.735Z" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186054770 1038 17592186549963 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186064194 1038 17592186549950 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186067318 1075 17592186067319 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186067318 1075 17592186067320 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186067318 1075 17592186067321 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186067318 1075 17592186067322 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186067318 1075 17592186067323 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186067318 1075 17592186549355 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186067318 1075 17592186549365 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186067318 1075 17592186549385 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186067318 1075 17592186549476 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186067318 1075 17592186549576 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186067318 1078 17592186549476 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186067714 1075 17592186067717 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186067714 1075 17592186549648 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186067714 1078 17592186067715 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186068848 1038 17592186549989 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186068874 1038 17592186549954 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186069358 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186069358 1071 "Solve the equation $3^{x} = \\frac{1}{27}$." 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186069358 1071 "Solve the equation ${3}^{x}=\\frac{1}{27}.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186069358 1074 "First, we rewrite $27$ as ${3}^{3}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {3}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}} \\\\ {3}^{x} & ={3}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x} & ={3}^{-3} \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-3.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186069424 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186069424 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{64}{b^{3}}}$." 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186069424 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{64}{{b}^{3}}}.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186069424 1074 "To simplify the expression, we write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{64}{{b}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-\\frac{4}{b})\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{b})\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{b})} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(-\\frac{4}{b})}^{3}} & = \\\\ -\\frac{4}{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186070091 1078 17592186070094 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186073673 1038 17592186550010 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186073674 1038 17592186549964 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186073675 1038 17592186549955 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186074001 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186074001 1071 "$4\\sqrt{3}-5\\sqrt{12}=$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186074001 1071 "What is $4\\sqrt{3} - 5\\sqrt{12}$?" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186074001 1074 "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{12} & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 3}=\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}-5\\sqrt{12} & = \\\\ 4\\sqrt{3}-5(2\\sqrt{3}) & = \\\\ 4\\sqrt{3}-10\\sqrt{3}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt{3}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}-10\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ (4-10)\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ -6\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078202 1071 "The first term of a geometric sequence is $2$, and the common ratio is $3$. What is the $9$th term?" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186078202 1071 "The first term of a geometric sequence is $2$, and the common ratio is $3.$ What is the $9$th term?" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078202 1074 "The first term is $a_1=2$, and the common ratio is $r=3$, so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $9$th term, we can use this formula with\n\n$$\nn={\\color{blue}9}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 2 \\cdot 3^{n - 1} \\\\\na_{9} &= 2 \\cdot 3^{9 - 1} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3^{8} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 6561 \\\\\n&= 13122\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is\n\n$$\na_{9}=13\\,122\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186078202 1074 "The first term is $a_1=2,$ and the common ratio is $r=3,$ so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $9$th term, we can use this formula with $n={\\color{blue}9}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{9} & =2\\cdot {3}^{9-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{8} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6561 \\\\  & =13\\,122\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is $a_{9}=13\\,122.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078203 1071 "Given that the $5$th term of a geometric sequence is $9$, and the common ratio is $3$, find the second term." 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186078203 1071 "Given that the $5$th term of a geometric sequence is $9$, and the common ratio is $3,$ find the second term." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078203 1074 "The common ratio is $r=3$, so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= a_{1} \\cdot 3^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term $a_1$. We know that the $5$th term is $a_5=9$, so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{5} &= a_{1} \\cdot 3^{5 - 1} \\\\\n9 &= a_{1} \\cdot 3^{4} \\\\\n9 &= a_{1} \\cdot 81 \\\\\n\\frac{9}{81} &= a_{1} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{9} &= a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is\n\n$$\na_1=\\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$\n\nwe can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot 3^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} \\cdot 3^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the second term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= \\frac{1}{9} \\cdot 3^{2 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second term is\n\n$$\na_2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186078203 1074 "The common ratio is $r=3,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term $a_1.$ We know that the $5$th term is $a_5=9,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5-1} \\\\ 9 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{4} \\\\ 9 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot 81 \\\\ \\frac{9}{81} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9} & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is $a_1=\\dfrac{1}{9},$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {3}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the second term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {3}^{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second term is $a_2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078204 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of a sequence if the $6$th term is $80$ and the common ratio is $-2$." 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186078204 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of a sequence if the $6$th term is $80$ and the common ratio is $-2.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078204 1074 "The common ratio is $r=-2$, so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= a_{1} \\cdot (-2)^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term $a_1$. We know that the $6$th term is $a_6=80$, so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{6} &= a_{1} \\cdot (-2)^{6 - 1} \\\\\n80 &= a_{1} \\cdot (-2)^{5} \\\\\n80 &= a_{1} \\cdot (-32) \\\\\n- \\frac{80}{32} &= a_{1} \\\\\n- \\frac{5}{2} &= a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is\n\n$$\na_1=-\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nwe can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot (-2)^{n - 1} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{2} \\cdot (-2)^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify a bit further by manipulating the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= -\\frac{5}{2}(-2)^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\cdot (-2)^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-2)^{-1} \\cdot (-2)^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-2)^{n - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186078204 1074 "The common ratio is $r=-2,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term $a_1.$ We know that the $6$th term is $a_6=80,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{6-1} \\\\ 80 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{5} \\\\ 80 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-32) \\\\ -\\frac{80}{32} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is $a_1=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify a bit further by manipulating the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{5}{2}(-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)^{-1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078531 1038 17592186550022 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078558 1038 17592186549981 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078558 1038 17592186550015 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078559 1038 17592186549940 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078559 1038 17592186549942 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078560 1038 17592186549992 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078560 1038 17592186549999 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078570 1038 17592186549949 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078570 1038 17592186549960 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078571 1038 17592186550004 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186078695 1050 600.0 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186079626 1064 "The general formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo illustrate how this works, suppose we want to find the $10$th term of the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n3, \\: 6, \\: 12, \\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nThe first term is $a_1=3$, and the common ratio is $r=2$, so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $10$th term, we can use this formula with\n\n$$\nn={\\color{blue}10}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 3 \\cdot 2^{n - 1} \\\\\na_{10} &= 3 \\cdot 2^{10 - 1} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2^{9} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 512 \\\\\n&= 1536\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is\n\n$$\na_{10}=1\\,536\n$$\n\nUsing the formula was much quicker than writing out all $10$ terms of the sequence!" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186079626 1064 "The general formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo illustrate how this works, suppose we want to find the $10$th term of the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n3, \\: 6, \\: 12, \\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nThe first term is $a_1=3,$ and the common ratio is $r=2,$ so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $10$th term, we can use this formula with $n={\\color{blue}10}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{10} & =3\\cdot {2}^{10-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{9} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 512 \\\\  & =1\\,536\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is $a_{10}=1\\,536.$ Using the formula was much quicker than writing out all $10$ terms of the sequence!" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186080915 1078 17592186080918 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186083161 1038 17592186550000 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186083161 1038 17592186550018 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186083664 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186083664 1071 "![](images/22/2280a258ec2cf548a5393772e497bece3ecbb61549c0d7260bad2146a9f3a094.png)\n\nThe graph of $f(x)$ is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $f^{-1}(x)$?" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186083664 1071 "![](images/22/2280a258ec2cf548a5393772e497bece3ecbb61549c0d7260bad2146a9f3a094.png)\n\nThe graph of $f(x)$ is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of ${f}^{-1}(x)?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186083664 1074 "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Of the given options, the only graph satisfying this condition is the one shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b2f729211c24879686601523f74935332dccbd6bf2e89b63af794b70dc721b1d.png)\n\nNotice that the points on $y=f(x)$ have corresponding points on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ with the coordinates swapped.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1,3) & ⟶(3,1) \\\\ (\\sqrt{3},9) & ⟶(9,\\sqrt{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186099339 1075 17592186099341 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186099339 1075 17592186099342 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186099339 1075 17592186099344 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186099339 1075 17592186549403 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186099339 1075 17592186549587 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186099339 1075 17592186549692 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186099339 1078 17592186549403 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186100527 1078 17592186100531 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186100849 1038 17592186549967 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186100849 1038 17592186549975 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186100946 1038 17592186549951 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186100946 1038 17592186549982 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186101355 1070 17592186045486 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186101355 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) - 3$. Which of the following points must lie on $y = f(x) + 1$?" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186101355 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-3.$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x)+1?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186101355 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)+1$ is obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)-3$ by $4$ units *up.* \n\nHence, if the point $(2,-1)$ lies on $y=f(x)-3$, then the point \n\n$$\n(2,-1+4)=(2,3)\n$$\n\nlies on $y=f(x)+1.$\n\n![](images/46/468f1e720d1a4834f1547b0cd2fcb8c9bff1e0342d2d98e33a62bbf9d473701a.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(2,-1)$ lies on $y=f(x)-3$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)-3 & =-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-1+3 \\\\ f(2) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $x=2$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2)+1 \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(2,3)$ must lie on $y=f(x)+1.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186101561 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186101561 1071 "![](images/64/642590d3e04de8365075f79f7954c5123019326b1f4addc618a9a2f54a09e881.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y = f(x)$. Which of the following gives the graph of $y = f(x - 1) + 3$?" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186101561 1071 "![](images/64/642590d3e04de8365075f79f7954c5123019326b1f4addc618a9a2f54a09e881.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(x-1)+3?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186101561 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(x-1)+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Translate it right by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$\n\n![](images/da/da5e6848776232c4f4429c8b3c5e3fdaaae2e9a99a180dfe409893e97d407036.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(x-1)+3.$\n\n![](images/81/8188a686d0f9c579f44601645667c0209a39b6500864eb9479147940ef1ebb26.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186110617 1038 17592186549995 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186110662 1038 17592186549971 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186110662 1038 17592186549985 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186110662 1038 17592186549990 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186115408 1038 17592186550012 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186119844 1038 17592186550021 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186119968 1038 17592186549931 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186119968 1038 17592186549933 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186119970 1038 17592186550001 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186121850 1075 17592186121851 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186121850 1075 17592186121852 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186121850 1075 17592186121853 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186121850 1075 17592186121854 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186121850 1075 17592186121855 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186121850 1075 17592186549338 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186121850 1075 17592186549439 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186121850 1075 17592186549503 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186121850 1075 17592186549619 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186121850 1075 17592186549764 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186121850 1078 17592186549619 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186124225 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=x^2+4x+6$?" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186124225 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=x^2+4x+6?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186124225 1074 "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny = ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwith $a=1>0$, $b=4$, and $c=6$.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} = -\\dfrac{4}{2 \\cdot 1} = -2\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-2$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{2} + 4x + 6 \\\\\n&= (-2)^{2} + 4(-2) + 6 \\\\\n&= 4 - 8 + 6 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-2,2)$, and the range of the function is $[2,\\infty)$.\n\n![](images/41/41b7219793120c0e37b3d910ef6a8f3754c1d9f27b784f937381743d7b7a85df.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186124225 1074 "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny = ax^2+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=1>0,$ $b=4,$ and $c=6.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx= -\\dfrac{b}{2a} = -\\dfrac{4}{2 \\cdot 1} = -2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-2$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}+4x+6 \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}+4(-2)+6 \\\\  & =4-8+6 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-2,2),$ and the range of the function is $[2,\\infty).$\n\n![](images/41/41b7219793120c0e37b3d910ef6a8f3754c1d9f27b784f937381743d7b7a85df.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186124226 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}(x+3)(x-1)$?" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186124226 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}(x+3)(x-1)?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186124226 1074 "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny = a(x-x_1)(x-x_2)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\na = -\\dfrac{1}{2} < 0\n$$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are $x_1=-3$ and $x_2=1$, and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} = \\frac{-3 + 1}{2} =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=-1$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -\\frac{1}{2}(x + 3)(x - 1) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}(-1 + 3)(-1 - 1) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot (-2) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-1,2)$, and the range of the function is $(-\\infty,2]$.\n\n![](images/2f/2f949680675dd0b0347adc154b8be6ce301ddb2f5b1b9ab0c077a5c00f81568f.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186124226 1074 "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny = a(x-x_1)(x-x_2),\n$$\n\nwith $a = -\\dfrac{1}{2} <    0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are $x_1=-3$ and $x_2=1,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{-3+1}{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=-1$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+3)(x-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1+3)(-1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-1,2),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty,2].$\n\n![](images/2f/2f949680675dd0b0347adc154b8be6ce301ddb2f5b1b9ab0c077a5c00f81568f.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186124391 1071 "What is the domain of the function $g(x)=\\dfrac{4}{3}x^2+5x -12$?" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186124391 1071 "What is the domain of the function $g(x)=\\dfrac{4}{3}x^2+5x -12?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186124391 1074 "A quadratic function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the domain of $g(x)$ is $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$." 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186124391 1074 "A quadratic function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the domain of $g(x)$ is $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186124393 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x) = (x+3)^2-2$?" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186124393 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x) = (x+3)^2-2?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186124393 1074 "The parabola is written in vertex form,\n\n$$\ny = a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex.\n\n- Since $h=-3$ and $k=-2$, the vertex is $(-3,-2)$.\n- Since $a=1>0$, the parabola opens upwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens upwards, the vertex corresponds to the minimum output, while there is no maximum output.\n\nTherefore, the range is $[-2,\\infty)$.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/579eef7814c2b51c44cded6fb2dc64e36d1106d359864e1e7ec7354511f80681.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186124393 1074 "The parabola is written in vertex form, \n\n$$\ny = a(x-h)^2 + k,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex. \n\n- Since $h=-3$ and $k=-2,$ the vertex is $(-3,-2).$\n\n- Since $a=1>0,$ the parabola opens upwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens upwards, the vertex corresponds to the minimum output, while there is no maximum output. \n\nTherefore, the range is $[-2,\\infty).$\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/579eef7814c2b51c44cded6fb2dc64e36d1106d359864e1e7ec7354511f80681.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186124542 1038 17592186549937 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186124552 1038 17592186549986 13194139535966 17