+1} \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{x+1})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)>-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>-1,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186193013 1070 17592186045486 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186193013 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = 3 - \\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}$." 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186193013 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186193013 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ x-3 & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ 3-x & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3-x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{y}}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{4}{3-x} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{4}{3-x})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{64}{(3-x)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{64}{(3-x)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 3,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186193825 1064 "In this lesson, we'll use our knowledge of solving radical equations to compute inverses of radical functions.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following radical function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\nTo compute the inverse of $f(x)$, we follow the usual four steps:\n\n**Step 1:** First, we replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx = 3\\sqrt{y} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Next, we solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 3\\sqrt{y} + 2 \\\\\n3\\sqrt{y} &= x - 2 \\\\\n\\sqrt{y} &= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 2) \\\\\n(\\sqrt{y})^{2} &= (\\frac{1}{3}(x - 2))^{2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{9}(x - 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Finally, we replace $y$ with $f^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x - 2)^2\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f(x) \\geq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq 0\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 false] [17592186193825 1064 "In this lesson, we'll use our knowledge of solving radical equations to compute inverses of radical functions. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following radical function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\nTo compute the inverse of $f(x),$ we follow the usual four steps:\n\n**Step 1:** First, we replace $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\sqrt{x} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we swap $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = 3\\sqrt{y} + 2\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Next, we solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{y}+2 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{y} & =x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{3}(x-2))}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Finally, we replace $y$ with $f^{-1}(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{9}(x - 2)^2\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0, \\qquad f(x) \\geq 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of $f^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2, \\qquad f^{-1}(x) \\geq 0.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186198378 1038 17592186549979 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186203428 1075 17592186549433 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186203428 1078 17592186549433 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186203429 1075 17592186549469 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [175