22 1075 17592186549418 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549922 1075 17592186549488 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549922 1075 17592186549622 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549922 1075 17592186549671 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549922 1076 17592186045493 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549922 1077 "selection" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549922 1078 17592186549671 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549922 1079 #uuid "fbc8d1ae-cf76-5d8c-94ef-a0c5ef612072" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549923 1075 17592186549535 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549923 1076 17592186045492 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549923 1077 "field-1" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549923 1078 17592186549535 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549923 1079 #uuid "fdceacd7-e832-59cd-b2a8-3585e4638dba" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549924 1068 17592186549863 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549924 1068 17592186549878 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549924 1069 "q-269466" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549924 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549924 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$760$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-40$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549924 1072 #uuid "078844cb-65ac-54f4-bde7-64e4bd44f313" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549924 1074 "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)=2{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}(2{y}^{T}y)=4y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=4{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i}.\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $4$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x,$ so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=4y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =4y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4([\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}76 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}760 & 760 \\\\ -40 & -40\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549925 1068 17592186549841 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549925 1069 "q-148570" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549925 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549925 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x+1}+k$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}.$ What is the value of $k?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549925 1072 #uuid "082ab1b4-7b0d-5301-97bc-d24a513a580b" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549925 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{3x+1}+k\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{3y+1}+k.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt[3]{3y+1}+k \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{3y+1} & =x-k \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{3y+1})}^{3} & ={(x-k)}^{3} \\\\ 3y+1 & =(x-k)^{3} \\\\ 3y & =(x-k)^{3}-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-k)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}(x-k)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-k=4\\,⟹\\,k=-4.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549926 1068 17592186549885 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549926 1069 "q-148082" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549926 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549926 1071 "A medical center is investigating the correlation between scoliosis and sports practice in a sample of children.\n\n- $25%$ of children in the sample don't practice sports, and $3%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $30%$ of children in the sample practice one sport, and $2.5%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $45%$ of children in the sample practice two sports, and $3.3%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\nIf $52%$ of children in the entire sample are female, and $2%$ of female children have scoliosis, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child with scoliosis is female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549926 1072 #uuid "095773d5-faca-5dcc-94ed-85e3db151ccc" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549926 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practice $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C|{S}_{0})=0.03 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 &  & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.033\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $52%$ of children in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.52.\n$$\n\n- Since $2%$ of females have scoliosis, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.02.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child with scoliosis is female, which is represented as $P(F|C).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{0})P({S}_{0})+P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.02)(0.52)}{(0.03)(0.25)+(0.025)(0.3)+(0.033)(0.45)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0104}{0.0299} \\\\  & ≈0.348,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549927 1068 17592186549907 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549927 1069 "q-148747" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549927 1070 17592186045486 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549927 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{x}}+2.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549927 1072 #uuid "0b14029b-c258-5cb1-9467-400506e4dea8" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549927 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{x}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{y}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{y}}+2 \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{1}{4(x-2)} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4(x-2)})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{64(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{64(x-2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549928 1068 17592186549902 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549928 1069 "q-35908" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549928 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549928 1071 "What is the range of the function $y=f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-3?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549928 1072 #uuid "0bc8d353-4f35-531e-9326-d4dcbc01bbdb" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549928 1074 "The parabola is written in vertex form, \n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex. \n\n- Since $h=1$ and $k=-3,$ the vertex is $(1,-3).$\n\n- Since $a=1>0,$ the parabola opens upwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens upwards, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output. \n\nTherefore, the range is $[-3,\\infty ).$\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/265532f4f4726151444ed873b875fc62c3c32a690a0ac859a318e2d4dc8fd285.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549929 1068 17592186549852 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549929 1068 17592186549886 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549929 1068 17592186549901 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549929 1069 "q-250654" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549929 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549929 1071 "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-2(x+1)(x-3).$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549929 1072 #uuid "0dff1477-477e-5350-b60a-a888d8f2c5e5" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549929 1074 "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-2<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=3,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{-1+3}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=1$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2(x+1)(x-3) \\\\  & =-2(1+1)(1-3) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(1,8),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,8],$ i.e. $-\\infty <f(x)\\le 8.$\n\n![](images/a9/a9c15f73f41c2c58fdbc05eceb3031edfb6f04f12db9fa54450f6445b196d1df.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549930 1068 17592186549797 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549930 1068 17592186549853 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549930 1068 17592186549883 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549930 1069 "q-250634" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549930 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549930 1071 "Find the range of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-6x+10.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$f(x)<$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549930 1072 #uuid "0eda0ab3-fc3e-57ae-8050-823c01afb804" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549930 1074 "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=1>0,$ $b=-6,$ and $c=10.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{(-6)}{2\\cdot 1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=3$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}-6x+10 \\\\  & =(3)^{2}-6(3)+10 \\\\  & =9-18+10 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(3,1),$ and the range of the function is $[1,\\infty ),$ i.e. $1\\le f(x)<\\infty .$\n\n![](images/4a/4abd9af6ea0eb83d2c99e4d02e67b09c27ea67576d0d006dbad56dff7f1d3e0e.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549931 1068 17592186549850 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549931 1069 "q-345102" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549931 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549931 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $45\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.01\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=15\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549931 1072 #uuid "0f78e83e-c080-5c56-8a40-9938a1632481" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549931 1074 "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/34/34ed425a41460e1fca35a028c2bd7add953d0b354b28a4d4f2ab02bab67a78a8.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=45\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=45I=45\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=15\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=15\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=15\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9=45\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q+15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{20}{3}q=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549932 1068 17592186549844 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549932 1069 "q-133851" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549932 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549932 1071 "![](images/8f/8fb46d101101d3d2bdb4afcfcc1436f1a62137389db2ffc3fde0cff71054e7f1.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nThe equation of the trend line is $y=27-0.5x.$ Assuming this trend continues, estimate the value of $y$ when $x=16.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549932 1072 #uuid "1131d502-6491-586e-9e5c-4aa6c2f2c8de" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549932 1074 "We can estimate the required value by substituting $x=16$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =27-0.5x \\\\  & =27-0.5(16) \\\\  & =27-8 \\\\  & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=16,$ we have $y≈19.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=16$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549933 1068 17592186549900 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549933 1069 "q-345116" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549933 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549933 1071 "![](images/22/22eff0b687ad9b417114aaf033cfe52186c884df00177c74ec7e85212b746c37.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549933 1072 #uuid "11a66deb-d8c3-5e00-97b4-215e597d7249" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549933 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $16\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=16\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=16I=16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=2\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8=16\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+8\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=4.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549934 1068 17592186549887 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549934 1069 "q-148431" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549934 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549934 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}-4.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549934 1072 #uuid "11b34dc5-01d5-59cc-afad-bce1d288e367" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549934 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x}-4\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{y}-4.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt[3]{y}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =x+4 \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & =(x+4)^{3} \\\\ y & =(x+4)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=(x+4)^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549935 1068 17592186549833 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549935 1069 "q-341830" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549935 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549935 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n}{2}x)\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of the $4\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5, & x\\in [0,2\\pi ) \\\\ -2, & x\\in [-2\\pi ,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{3}.$\n\n${b}_{3}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549935 1072 #uuid "14280c74-af6e-5d18-9712-69501b60ddc5" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549935 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2\\pi .$ Also, $f(x)=5$ for $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $f(x)=-2$ for $x\\in [-2\\pi ,0).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{-2\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{-2\\pi }^{0}(-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{2\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3}){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x)]}_{-2\\pi }^{0}+\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3}){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3\\pi ))-\\frac{5}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }(1-(-1))-\\frac{5}{3\\pi }((-1)-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }+\\frac{10}{3\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549936 1068 17592186549840 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549936 1069 "q-277826" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549936 1070 17592186045486 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549936 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-1}}+2.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549936 1072 #uuid "14c0067f-8b9b-586e-a9fe-ecef26e510a6" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549936 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-1}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-1}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-1}}+2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y-1} & =\\frac{3}{x-2} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{2y-1})^{3} & ={(\\frac{3}{x-2})}^{3} \\\\ 2y-1 & =\\frac{27}{(x-2)^{3}} \\\\ 2y & =\\frac{27}{(x-2)^{3}}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{27}{2(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{27}{2(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{1}{2},\\,f(x)\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549937 1068 17592186549906 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549937 1069 "q-97580" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549937 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549937 1071 "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549937 1072 #uuid "15721919-affc-527a-806f-d54311da77ac" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549937 1074 "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549938 1068 17592186549823 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549938 1069 "q-148271" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549938 1070 17592186045486 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549938 1071 "A medical center is investigating the correlation between scoliosis and sports practice in a sample of children.\n\n- $25%$ of children in the sample don't practice sports, and $2.5%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $30%$ of children in the sample practice one sport.\n\n- $45%$ of children in the sample practice two sports, and $2.8%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\nIf $48%$ of children in the entire sample are female, $2.6%$ of female children have scoliosis, and $46%$ of children with scoliosis are female, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child who practices one sport has scoliosis? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549938 1072 #uuid "1787d264-9d95-57d2-8330-7eec4c052cfe" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549938 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practices $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C∣{S}_{0})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C∣{S}_{2})=0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $48%$ of students in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.48.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.6%$ of females have scoliosis, we have\n\n$$\nP(C∣F)=0.026.\n$$\n\n- Since $46%$ of children with scoliosis are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F∣C)=0.46.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child who practices one sport has scoliosis, which is represented as $P(C∣{S}_{1}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F∣C) & =\\frac{P(C∣F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\ P(F∣C) & =\\frac{P(C∣F)P(F)}{P(C∣{S}_{0})P({S}_{0})+P(C∣{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C∣{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})} \\\\ 0.46 & =\\frac{(0.026)(0.48)}{(0.25)(0.025)+P(C∣{S}_{1})(0.3)+(0.028)(0.45)} \\\\ 0.46 & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(C∣{S}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.46(0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1})) & =0.0125 \\\\ 0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.46} \\\\ 0.3P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.46}-0.0189 \\\\ P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.3}(\\frac{0.0125}{0.46}-0.0189) \\\\ P(C∣{S}_{1}) & ≈0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549939 1068 17592186549851 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549939 1069 "q-277054" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549939 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549939 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=5\\sqrt[3]{x}+3.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549939 1072 #uuid "1883917d-29b4-5836-94bf-f6ba9fe053f5" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549939 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=5\\sqrt[3]{x}+3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=5\\sqrt[3]{y}+3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\sqrt[3]{y}+3 \\\\ 5\\sqrt[3]{y} & =x-3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x-3) \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{5}(x-3))}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{125}(x-3)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{125}(x-3)^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549940 1068 17592186549877 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549940 1069 "q-5450" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549940 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549940 1071 "Find the equation for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence if the $6$th term is $-\\frac{1}{5}$ and the common ratio is $-\\frac{1}{5}.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549940 1072 #uuid "18bc1e21-0c4b-52f3-824f-e719cd86e034" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549940 1074 "The common ratio is $r=-\\frac{1}{5},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $6$th term is ${a}_{6}=-\\frac{1}{5},$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{6-1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{5} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & =-{a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{5} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & =-{a}_{1}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{5}^{5}}) \\\\ {5}^{4} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 625 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=625,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =625\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549941 1068 17592186549822 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549941 1069 "q-133883" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549941 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549941 1071 "![](images/df/dff61f08e09f21935f616788e54dfb56e0d0a89f38cce9fbc6dc7333d21ba5f1.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nUse the trend line to predict the value of $x$ when $y=80.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549941 1072 #uuid "1b530d38-bb33-510e-b9cb-65e2c39387f5" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549941 1074 "To make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $y=80$ on the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/e5/e5923b6c9f0ac4dcf2aee35e67877c425b44f81830bb9f0865c5e21acf81e3f4.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/61/613849314df8014335f7493f9b6b64cb4f2ecd1e585593416f416f1854e12ffa.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/45/4523e742266e3f2d2e759c6e65eb2a7f2bd31e8f4b8473b8e6bfbabec067c9d9.png)\n\nTherefore, when $y=80,$ we obtain $x≈15.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549942 1068 17592186549870 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549942 1069 "q-5449" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549942 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549942 1071 "Find the equation for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence if the $5$th term is $16$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549942 1072 #uuid "1bbae00a-a76f-5b1d-a767-0acf7498bde8" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549942 1074 "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{2}{3},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=16,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5-1} \\\\ 16 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4} \\\\ {2}^{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{4}}{{3}^{4}}) \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{4}\\cdot {3}^{4}}{{2}^{4}} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {3}^{4} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 81 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=81,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =81\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549943 1068 17592186549802 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549943 1068 17592186549831 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549943 1068 17592186549869 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549943 1068 17592186549912 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549943 1069 "q-355247" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549943 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549943 1071 "| $t$ | $h(t)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-5$ | $4$ |\n| $-2$ | $-1$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |\n| $6$ | $-6$ |\n\nThe table above gives values of a function $h$ on the set $T=\\{-5,-2,3,6\\}.$ Compute the following:\n\n${max}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n${min}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{t\\in T}h(t)=$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549943 1072 #uuid "1bfb5188-7c59-596d-bb0b-41e90d79e407" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549943 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the maximum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that maximum output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- ${min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the minimum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that minimum output occurs.\n\nThe output values are $4,-1,7,$ and $-6.$ The maximum output is $7$ (at $t=3$), and the minimum output is $-6$ (at $t=6$). \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{max}_{t\\in T}h(t) & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{t\\in T}h(t) & =3 \\\\ {min}_{t\\in T}h(t) & =-6 \\\\ \\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{t\\in T}h(t) & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549944 1068 17592186549828 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549944 1069 "q-277956" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549944 1070 17592186045486 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549944 1071 "The function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8x+3}}+2,\\,x>-\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{2(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{3}{8},\\,x>2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$ Assume that $k>0.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549944 1072 #uuid "22e0c565-a427-5cb0-889a-a9c4e5867def" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549944 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8x+3}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8y+3}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8y+3}}+2 \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8y+3}} \\\\ \\sqrt{8y+3} & =\\frac{k}{x-2} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{8y+3})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{k}{x-2})}^{2} \\\\ 8y+3 & =\\frac{{k}^{2}}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ 8y & =\\frac{{k}^{2}}{(x-2)^{2}}-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{k}^{2}}{8(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{k}^{2}}{8(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{3}{8},\\,x>2.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{k}^{2}}{8}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=6.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549945 1068 17592186549800 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549945 1069 "q-90889" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549945 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549945 1071 "What is the domain of the function $h(x)=\\frac{2}{3}(x+4)^{2}-6?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549945 1072 #uuid "25370e2a-dfa1-5d6f-b02b-964c5925c1f3" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549945 1074 "A quadratic function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the domain of $h(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549946 1068 17592186549903 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549946 1069 "q-152903" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549946 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549946 1071 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[9X+4].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{2},\\,0<x<2\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549946 1072 #uuid "2595de8f-014d-5db6-a5d3-e22363b7564e" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549946 1074 "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[9X+4]=9\\text{E}[X]+4\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[9X+4] & =9\\text{E}[X]+4 \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+4 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549947 1068 17592186549894 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549947 1069 "q-148496" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549947 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549947 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x-3}.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549947 1072 #uuid "286413cf-e05b-52a9-9010-9fd10625bdfe" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549947 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x-3}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y-3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y-3} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y-3} & =3x \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{y-3})}^{3} & ={(3x)}^{3} \\\\ y-3 & =27{x}^{3} \\\\ y & =27{x}^{3}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=27{x}^{3}+3.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549948 1068 17592186549855 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549948 1069 "q-90971" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549948 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549948 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-3{x}^{2}-12x-17?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549948 1072 #uuid "28ef6032-c464-5039-b5ee-d8d9a6314099" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549948 1074 "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=-3<0,$ $b=-12,$ and $c=-17.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{(-12)}{2\\cdot (-3)}=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-2$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3{x}^{2}-12x-17 \\\\  & =-3(-2)^{2}-12(-2)-17 \\\\  & =-12+24-17 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-2,-5),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-5].$\n\n![](images/3c/3c62a8826f68c3c36db191769fc23907ca6c64986bc1f47b5a7b21b2b97a91f2.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549949 1068 17592186549837 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549949 1069 "q-251017" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549949 1070 17592186045486 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549949 1071 "Evaluate ${\\sum }_{m=2}^{5}\\frac{2m+1}{10}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549949 1072 #uuid "2ac819b8-8252-5f38-81b6-27fb48d49f54" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549949 1074 "The sum takes integer values of $m$ from $2$ to $5.$ So, computing the terms for $m=2,3,4,5$ and then adding them, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{m=2}^{5}\\frac{2m+1}{10} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2+1}{10}+\\frac{2\\cdot 3+1}{10}+\\frac{2\\cdot 4+1}{10}+\\frac{2\\cdot 5+1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{7}{10}+\\frac{9}{10}+\\frac{11}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+7+9+11}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 16}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549950 1068 17592186549796 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549950 1069 "q-215096" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549950 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549950 1071 "If $v=2τdh,$ then $τ=${{field-1}}." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549950 1072 #uuid "2c796978-9336-5fc7-a4cc-bd1fe1ccd4cd" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549950 1074 "We need to isolate $τ,$ so we will treat the other variables $v,d,$ and $h$ as constants. We can use the multiplication principle to solve for $τ.$\n\nFirst, we rearrange and then divide both sides by $2dh:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =2τdh \\\\ v & =(2dh)τ \\\\ \\frac{v}{2dh} & =\\frac{(2dh)τ}{2dh} \\\\ \\frac{v}{2dh} & =τ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $τ=\\frac{v}{2dh}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $d\\ne 0$ and $h\\ne 0.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549951 1068 17592186549920 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549951 1069 "q-112634" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549951 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549951 1071 "![](images/ec/ec193bb78fbfb43cec5266622ef0bef14e6aad5798687ae9c7cc7233e69078cb.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(3(x-5))?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549951 1072 #uuid "311d3c6d-fd7c-5156-9816-24d4c0a4cb73" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549951 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(3(x-5)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(3)}=\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(3x).$\n\n![](images/c4/c41d06f741882e3993ebe8eb7eab5c42c362d3c11b9b8ad1e4fed983498c95d0.png)\n\n- Then, translate it right by $5$ units to get the graph of $y=f(3(x-5)).$\n\n![](images/47/47bf38d228b4337b870baf777c53fbab43743f155f4a23aa6d0e2bd1f5bc45ad.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549952 1068 17592186549812 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549952 1069 "q-269370" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549952 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549952 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549952 1072 #uuid "34c44cc9-bf3e-5025-8ef7-d8fd637a6eae" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549952 1074 "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14 & 14 & -28 \\\\ 14 & 14 & -28\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549953 1068 17592186549825 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549953 1069 "q-148501" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549953 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549953 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=2\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549953 1072 #uuid "37674798-c6eb-521f-b1bd-86df515c51cc" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549953 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt[3]{3y-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt[3]{3y-1} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{3y-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{3y-1})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{3} \\\\ 3y-1 & =\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3} \\\\ 3y & =\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}+1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{24}({x}^{3}+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{24}({x}^{3}+8).\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549954 1068 17592186549804 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549954 1069 "q-140285" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549954 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549954 1071 "Rewrite the expression $({y}^{2}-24y+144)+5$ as the sum of a perfect square and a constant." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549954 1072 #uuid "3846b0e4-266a-5fcc-97c9-5ac90ce8b7bf" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549954 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2ay+{a}^{2}=(y-a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-24y+144 & = \\\\ {y}^{2}-24y+{12}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}-2\\cdot 12y+{12}^{2} & = \\\\ (y-12)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}-24y+144)+5 & = \\\\ (y-12)^{2}+5 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549955 1068 17592186549827 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549955 1069 "q-76841" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549955 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549955 1071 "If $m∠A=(3x+1)^{∘}$ and $m∠B=(4x-9)^{∘},$ find a value of $x$ that satisfies $\\operatorname{cot}⁡A=\\operatorname{tan}⁡B.$  Suppose that both $∠A$ and $∠B$ are acute." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549955 1072 #uuid "3a13b69c-0da2-5493-86d2-76c8bc977240" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549955 1074 "If both angles are acute and $\\operatorname{cot}⁡A=\\operatorname{tan}⁡B,$ then the angles $∠A$ and $∠B$ must be complementary, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x+1)^{∘}+(4x-9)^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ (7x-8)^{∘} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ 7x-8 & =90 \\\\ 7x & =98 \\\\ x & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549956 1068 17592186549830 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549956 1069 "q-277067" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549956 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549956 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=2-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{x}}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549956 1072 #uuid "3c4a5911-5181-51eb-9128-55f7bf7227a5" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549956 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}} & =2-x \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{6(2-x)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{6(2-x)})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{36(2-x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{36(2-x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)<2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx<2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549957 1068 17592186549905 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549957 1068 17592186549909 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549957 1069 "q-341272" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549957 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549957 1071 "Let $F(x)=7x-4$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(-4+\\frac{7\\pi }{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549957 1072 #uuid "3fb491ae-d461-5efd-a743-cfec479bf958" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549957 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=7x-4$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(7x-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=7x-4\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=7 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(7x-4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{7}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((7\\pi -4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{7}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{7}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{14(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{14((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(-4+\\frac{7\\pi }{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{14((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549958 1068 17592186549801 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549958 1069 "q-133920" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549958 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549958 1071 "![](images/b5/b5d153746d837a857918028aceb623a5c2b9267f00b6baaceb2f24a8172587ee.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nUse the trend line to predict the value of $y$ when $x=70.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549958 1072 #uuid "44c1c5d8-2db1-5cbc-a653-89f6c7b7e591" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549958 1074 "To make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $x=70$ on the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/ee/ee670899ebf962939a77959c58219502907681d8cf84329f402b67f6f09a24ba.png)\n\n* Then, we move up until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/f6/f6d34d0c6b95b4b3d5ecf720346ca1e0008ac86c42e8024a2435e2bf792e5031.png)\n\n- Finally, we move left until we intersect the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/02/0217edcb171859f1aec207d5c248c483a3147dbe74aed277f1313009dbc317c9.png)\n\nTherefore, when $x=70,$ we obtain $y≈5.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549959 1068 17592186549846 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549959 1069 "q-341861" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549959 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549959 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-4x$ for $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{2}.$\n\n${b}_{2}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549959 1072 #uuid "46598e7e-81d3-578b-80cc-94d59f9707e8" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549959 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=\\pi $ and $f(x)=-4x$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{\\pi }x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-4x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-4 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{2}{[(-4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(-4)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{2}((-4\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-(4\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi ))-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{2}((-4\\pi )\\cdot 1-(4\\pi )\\cdot 1)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(4\\pi -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot (4\\pi -0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549960 1068 17592186549921 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549960 1069 "q-5338" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549960 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549960 1071 "Evaluate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}5\\cdot {2}^{n-1}.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549960 1072 #uuid "470997c7-250a-5a72-adc9-b7b406031a9e" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549960 1074 "The sum takes integer values of $n$ from $1$ to $3.$ So, computing the terms for $n=1,2,3$ and then adding them, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}5\\cdot {2}^{n-1} & =(5\\cdot {2}^{1-1})+(5\\cdot {2}^{2-1})+(5\\cdot {2}^{3-1}) \\\\  & =(5\\cdot {2}^{0})+(5\\cdot {2}^{1})+(5\\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 1)+(5\\cdot 2)+(5\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =5+10+20 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549961 1068 17592186549843 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549961 1069 "q-103266" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549961 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549961 1071 "![](images/4f/4f538bb715c53ad0478539feaef29c725f6f6e494134726faede38586e60a4d6.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the angles shown above?\n\nI. $m∠AOB=m∠BOC$\n\nII. $m∠BOC=m∠COD$\n\nIII. $∠AOB≅∠DOE$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549961 1072 #uuid "47312f69-f326-5376-9041-14157a2dd5a5" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549961 1074 "From the diagram, we see that \n\n- $∠AOB$ and $∠BOC$ are marked with $|$, and\n\n- $∠COD$ and $∠DOE$ are marked with $||.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠AOB & ≅∠BOC \\\\ ∠COD & ≅∠DOE.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB & =m∠BOC \\\\ m∠COD & =m∠DOE.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo statement I is true, while statements II and III are false." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549962 1068 17592186549867 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549962 1068 17592186549874 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549962 1069 "q-341270" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549962 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549962 1071 "Let $F(x)=4x-1$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(-1+2\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549962 1072 #uuid "55d2ffed-9ece-55ed-baea-5ef5a75af131" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549962 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=4x-1$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(4x-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4x-1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=4 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(4x-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((4\\pi -1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{4}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{4}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{8((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(-1+2\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549963 1068 17592186549817 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549963 1068 17592186549882 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549963 1069 "q-171612" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549963 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549963 1071 "The solution to the inequality $-2a\\ge 10$ is $a${{field-1}}{{field-2}}." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549963 1072 #uuid "5c6ca484-3f17-5181-8810-930cfb1991ba" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549963 1074 "The variable $a$ is being *multiplied* by $-2.$ To isolate $a,$ we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-2.$ Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\nAlso, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2a\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,10 \\\\ \\frac{-2a}{-2}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,\\frac{10}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{-2a}{-2}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-2$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2a}{-2} & \\le -5 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-2}a}{\\cancel{-2}} & \\le -5 \\\\ a & \\le -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a\\le -5.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549964 1068 17592186549835 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549964 1069 "q-76838" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549964 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549964 1071 "$\\operatorname{cot}⁡({27}^{∘}-\\theta )=$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549964 1072 #uuid "5cb3bde9-32a4-52cd-9e8b-d6d4c061a2e8" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549964 1074 "We know that the cofunction of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta $ is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ,$ and the angle complementary to ${27}^{∘}-\\theta $ is \n\n$$\n{90}^{∘}-({27}^{∘}-\\theta )={90}^{∘}-{27}^{∘}+\\theta ={63}^{∘}+\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡({27}^{∘}-\\theta )=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({90}^{∘}-({27}^{∘}-\\theta ))=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({63}^{∘}+\\theta ).\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549965 1068 17592186549910 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549965 1069 "q-277959" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549965 1070 17592186045486 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549965 1071 "The function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6x-1}}+2,\\,x\\ne \\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{81(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{6},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549965 1072 #uuid "5cb5bb60-d097-50b4-9ba5-bde88e86a4d3" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549965 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6x-1}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6y-1}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6y-1}}+2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6y-1}} & =x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{6y-1} & =\\frac{2}{k(x-2)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{6y-1})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{k(x-2)})}^{3} \\\\ 6y-1 & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}} \\\\ 6y & =\\frac{8}{{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{3{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{6},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3{k}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{81}\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549966 1068 17592186549892 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549966 1069 "q-277840" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549966 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549966 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{x-5}+6$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=16(x-6)^{2}+5.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549966 1072 #uuid "635cbae5-da30-5f6f-818b-8857829587ba" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549966 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{x-5}+6\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{y-5}+6.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{y-5}+6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{y-5} & =x-6 \\\\ \\sqrt{y-5} & =k(x-6) \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y-5})}^{2} & ={(k(x-6))}^{2} \\\\ y-5 & ={k}^{2}(x-6)^{2} \\\\ y & ={k}^{2}(x-6)^{2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={k}^{2}(x-6)^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,k=4.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 5,\\,f(x)\\ge 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 6,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 5.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549967 1068 17592186549836 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549967 1069 "q-26056" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549967 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549967 1071 "The point $Q$ with coordinates $(-1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$ and the point $R$ with coordinates $(-1,-2)$ lies on the curve $y=g(x).$ If the curve $y=g(x)$ is related to $y=f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x)=f(x)+k$, then what is the value of $k?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549967 1072 #uuid "6bd4c5ad-d5d4-5f1c-b1a3-32f3fc367847" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549967 1074 "First, notice that the point $R(-1,-2)$ lies $3$ units below the point $Q(-1,1).$ So, the graph of $y=g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by shifting it $3$ units down. \n\n![](images/7d/7d470919b374a5796b49ff81f893f37f5bb76afd4546a25459259f3b6339529b.png)\n\nAs a result, $k=-3.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549968 1068 17592186549923 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549968 1069 "q-341274" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549968 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549968 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=5{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549968 1072 #uuid "6c123c90-9e3c-534f-a040-61cb255aa0e6" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549968 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=5{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(5{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{5{\\pi }^{3}}{3}+\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549969 1068 17592186549875 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549969 1069 "q-341277" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549969 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549969 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=6{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549969 1072 #uuid "6da2a50a-0f62-59bb-a9db-a784f084c0f1" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549969 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=6{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(6{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[2{x}^{3}+x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(2{\\pi }^{3}+\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2(2{\\pi }^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549970 1068 17592186549889 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549970 1069 "q-133962" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549970 1070 17592186045486 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549970 1071 "![](images/6f/6f38e64442bb383f218dede5a88740653f958864d3284ce09c3e1bb99ed7e421.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the time (in hours) some college students spend studying the night before an exam and the time they spend sleeping that night. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the time a student spends studying if that student gets only $3.75$ hours of sleep.\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(2,7.5)$ and $(4,5).$*" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549970 1072 #uuid "73f7b139-c8da-5c53-9a35-10e77ca9a819" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549970 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/9e/9e1653b2acbe5c5924e2d562308b2640c64f317ed7500fba4e149c926c9205ab.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,7.5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,5).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-7.5}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2.5)}{2} \\\\  & =-1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-1.25$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-1.25x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1.25x+b \\\\ 5 & =-1.25(4)+b \\\\ 5 & =-5+b \\\\ b & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-1.25x+10.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=3.75$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1.25x+10 \\\\ 3.75 & =-1.25x+10 \\\\ 1.25x & =6.25 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the student spends approximately $5$ hours studying.\n\nNote: The point where $y=3.75$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549971 1068 17592186549914 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549971 1069 "q-2648" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549971 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549971 1071 "If $x=\\frac{81{a}^{8}}{{b}^{2}{c}^{3}}$ and $y=\\frac{{b}^{7}{c}^{11}}{9{a}^{4}},$ then $xy=$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549971 1072 #uuid "759e1619-eead-5907-8f58-8d6fbf13ba52" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549971 1074 "First, we substitute for $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & =(\\frac{81{a}^{8}}{{b}^{2}{c}^{3}})\\cdot (\\frac{{b}^{7}{c}^{11}}{9{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{81{a}^{8}{b}^{7}{c}^{11}}{9{a}^{4}{b}^{2}{c}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the fraction, group the three variables separately, and apply the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{81{a}^{8}{b}^{7}{c}^{11}}{9{a}^{4}{b}^{2}{c}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{81}{9}\\cdot \\frac{{a}^{8}}{{a}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{{b}^{7}}{{b}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{c}^{11}}{{c}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{81}{9}\\cdot {a}^{(8-4)}\\cdot {b}^{(7-2)}\\cdot {c}^{(11-3)} & = \\\\ 9{a}^{4}{b}^{5}{c}^{8} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549972 1068 17592186549865 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549972 1069 "q-269369" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549972 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549972 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549972 1072 #uuid "7c674592-16b0-5e5a-b0ac-ce1e62f430e7" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549972 1074 "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}2{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=2(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}])=2([\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 14 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 28 & -14 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549973 1068 17592186549908 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549973 1069 "q-90974" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549973 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549973 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-4x+4?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549973 1072 #uuid "7dfd6f87-7029-50ed-aece-aeae8cb98e8c" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549973 1074 "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=2>0,$ $b=-4,$ and $c=4.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{(-4)}{2\\cdot 2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=1$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{x}^{2}-4x+4 \\\\  & =2(1)^{2}-4(1)+4 \\\\  & =2-4+4 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(1,2),$ and the range of the function is $[2,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/5f/5f98a1b646aeab2a8a0f0a04014abb0a0a88794d88c880fb771d5f2f65afa4e7.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549974 1068 17592186549881 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549974 1069 "q-148550" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549974 1070 17592186045486 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549974 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}}.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549974 1072 #uuid "7e395659-7f2e-5dda-a6c4-f51ac9defcd5" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549974 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}} \\\\ x-2 & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}} \\\\ 2-x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{3y+2} & =\\frac{1}{2-x} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{3y+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2-x})}^{2} \\\\ 3y+2 & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ 3y & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>-\\frac{2}{3},\\,f(x)<2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx<2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549975 1068 17592186549814 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549975 1069 "q-86632" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549975 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549975 1071 "The point $Q$ with coordinates $(-2,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$ and the point $R$ with coordinates $(-2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y=g(x).$ If the curve $y=g(x)$ is related to $y=f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x)=f(x)+k$, then what is the value of $k?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549975 1072 #uuid "841b4d8f-31c4-51b2-96fd-34cf02a240b8" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549975 1074 "First, notice that the point $R(-2,-1)$ lies $2$ units below the point $Q(-2,1).$ So, the graph of $y=g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by shifting it $2$ units down. \n\n![](images/15/158488a446217332d0b70d2e6b116bc1e17e7e5b205ff5ebe4289a75ef05fa05.png)\n\nAs a result, $k=-2.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549976 1068 17592186549849 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549976 1068 17592186549873 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549976 1068 17592186549895 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549976 1068 17592186549911 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549976 1069 "q-355233" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549976 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549976 1071 "![](images/4b/4bda4bc11eb5d030d3c9db2336fd8ba98b4c994f2e19f040774225fd275b07e5.png)\n\nLet $T=\\{0,2,3,4,5\\}.$ The graph above shows the value of $h(x)$ for each $x\\in T.$ Assuming that all the dots have integer coordinates, compute the following:\n\n${max}_{x\\in T}h(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in T}h(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n${min}_{x\\in T}h(x)=$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in T}h(x)=$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549976 1072 #uuid "872e3e1c-801d-5da3-9c5c-97c11a673f6e" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549976 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the maximum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that maximum output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- ${min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the minimum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that minimum output occurs.\n\nFrom the graph, \n\n- the highest point is at $(2,3),$ so ${max}_{x\\in T}h(x)=3$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in T}h(x)=2,$ and\n\n- the lowest point is at $(3,-2),$ so ${min}_{x\\in T}h(x)=-2$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in T}h(x)=3.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549977 1068 17592186549922 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549977 1069 "q-133932" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549977 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549977 1071 "![](images/31/31932ff71e679d32ba3abee0276f7a039e5f204649e63feffeb13029eabc5b88.png)\n\nAn ice cream shop wants to determine the relationship between the outside temperature $x$ (in degrees Celsius) and the number of customers $y$ they serve. The scatter plot above shows the information they collected over several days. The equation of the trend line is $y=2.25x-19.$\n\nEstimate the outside temperature if they served $53$ customers." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549977 1072 #uuid "8bba2235-837e-5ee6-9f31-af7dd59da4b8" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549977 1074 "To predict the outside temperature knowing that they served $53$ customers, we need to substitute $y=53$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.25x-19 \\\\ 53 & =2.25x-19 \\\\ 2.25x & =72 \\\\ x & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, the outside temperature is approximately ${32}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=53$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549978 1068 17592186549880 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549978 1069 "q-341863" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549978 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549978 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=x+1$ for $x\\in [-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{2}.$\n\n${a}_{2}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549978 1072 #uuid "8c3f231c-8d0c-511e-b4f9-0d81c5e6659f" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549978 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=x+1$ for $x\\in [-2,2).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(uv\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }{[(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)]}_{-2}^{2}-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }[3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )]-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\pi }){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)]}_{-2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi )]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549979 1068 17592186549842 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549979 1069 "q-161305" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549979 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549979 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,xz⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{v}^{2},\\,uv⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}a{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+bu{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549979 1072 #uuid "8e8e30b7-68c9-50ac-bfbf-42853c6a4c1f" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549979 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & v \\\\ 2v & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-u,\\,2v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,xz⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y={v}^{2}\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u,\\,{v}^{2},\\,{u}^{2}v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{v}^{2},\\,-u,\\,2v⟩ \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-2{v}^{2})+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-u)+{u}^{2}v\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =-2u{v}^{2}-u{v}^{2}+2{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}-3u{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\cdot {u}^{2}{v}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=2-3=-1.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549980 1068 17592186549810 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549980 1068 17592186549815 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549980 1068 17592186549839 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549980 1068 17592186549854 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549980 1068 17592186549904 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549980 1069 "q-341780" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549980 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549980 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in [-L,L)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({{field-1}})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({{field-2}}).\n$$\n\nThe $7$th sine coefficient of the series is\n\n$$\n{b}_{7}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $F(x)=x$ and $L=2\\pi ,$ the $7$th sine coefficient becomes\n\n$$\n{b}_{7}={{field-4}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-5}}.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549980 1072 #uuid "903d90ff-4e96-59f7-912a-24572f1e9b78" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549980 1074 "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k\\ne 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}=2\\pi .$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7$th sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{7} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=2\\pi $ and $f(x)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{7} & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{2\\pi }x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7}{2}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549981 1068 17592186549891 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549981 1069 "q-265737" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549981 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549981 1071 "The first term of a geometric sequence is $-\\frac{1}{81},$ and the common ratio is $-3.$ The $9$th term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549981 1072 #uuid "91775cc6-b3ff-5403-8a6d-5a5294e1af4c" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549981 1074 "The first term is ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{81},$ and the common ratio is $r=-3,$ so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot (-3)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $9$th term, we can use this formula with $n=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot (-3)^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{9} & =-\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot (-3)^{9-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot (-3)^{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}}\\cdot {3}^{8} \\\\  & =-{3}^{4} \\\\  & =-81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is ${a}_{9}=-81.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549982 1068 17592186549890 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549982 1069 "q-87243" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549982 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549982 1071 "![](images/fa/fa8eef8fc185109d90dcc6c17f9684db06050d9b2cf63560186c45e8ce0ac204.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(3(x+2))?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549982 1072 #uuid "9683153e-7324-5fb1-80ce-172326e0407d" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549982 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(3(x+2)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(3)}=\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(3x).$\n\n![](images/30/3027f0943e238d63996c8d1ff36d36b14bb60c18957168f7e54bb0a7ddcd9d33.png)\n\n  \n\n- Then, translate it left by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=f(3(x+2)).$\n\n![](images/e3/e3e552a5bacd539ee409a69be7e69cddc4e4e2404bcc4adc5173e980e8c8ca93.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549983 1068 17592186549896 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549983 1069 "q-106278" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549983 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549983 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)=(x-4)^{2}+19?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549983 1072 #uuid "9acd8689-6770-5d1b-9670-f2ff78d7e31a" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549983 1074 "The parabola is written in vertex form, \n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex. \n\n- Since $h=4$ and $k=19,$ the vertex is $(4,19).$\n\n- Since $a=1>0,$ the parabola opens upwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens upwards, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nTherefore, the range is $[19,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/7f/7fbc9dc7839e439967d6f9f50e25d561ff7be8211b899c83ff269b019b4c61c5.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549984 1068 17592186549819 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549984 1069 "q-147885" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549984 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549984 1071 "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3},{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.25, & P({A}_{1})=0.28 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.3, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.28, & P({A}_{3})=0.18 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{4})=0.1, & P({A}_{4})=0.22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549984 1072 #uuid "9c281bbc-e5bb-5c0b-9b81-0eabf770ee2e" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549984 1074 "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=4,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}) & =1-(P({A}_{1})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})) \\\\  & =1-(0.28+0.18+0.22) \\\\  & =0.32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})+P(B|{A}_{4})P({A}_{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.3)(0.32)}{(0.25)(0.28)+(0.3)(0.32)+(0.28)(0.18)+(0.1)(0.22)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.096}{0.2384} \\\\  & ≈0.403.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549985 1068 17592186549826 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549985 1069 "q-424" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549985 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549985 1071 "${(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}})}^{3}=$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549985 1072 #uuid "9cb24353-5541-5833-aaa7-2a27c3864cfb" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549985 1074 "We start by using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}})}^{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{(3y)^{3}}{{({x}^{2})}^{3}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power of product rule in the numerator and the power rule in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3y)^{3}}{{({x}^{2})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{3}^{3}{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2\\cdot 3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{27{y}^{3}}{{x}^{6}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549986 1068 17592186549897 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549986 1069 "q-83392" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549986 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549986 1071 "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-23 & -15 \\\\ 9 & 19\\end{aligned}],$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549986 1072 #uuid "a0915342-2d27-50ea-9893-ae57d8dcc177" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549986 1074 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A)=-23+19=-4.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549987 1068 17592186549798 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549987 1068 17592186549808 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549987 1068 17592186549899 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549987 1068 17592186549918 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549987 1069 "q-355240" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549987 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549987 1071 "![](images/dc/dc692d5337c00f1cca52fe3020d26a2859288c257f0a049e3f6b7f159353d7ce.png)\n\nLet $T=\\{0,1,3,4\\}.$ The graph above shows the value of $g(t)$ for each $t\\in T.$ Assuming that all the dots have integer coordinates, compute the following:\n\n${max}_{t\\in T}g(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{t\\in T}g(t)=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n${min}_{t\\in T}g(t)=$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{t\\in T}g(t)=$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549987 1072 #uuid "aa3208c3-dd8f-52a7-9e52-31f81d41b1b4" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549987 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the maximum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that maximum output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- ${min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the minimum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that minimum output occurs.\n\nFrom the graph, \n\n- the highest point is at $(0,3),$ so ${max}_{t\\in T}g(t)=3$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{t\\in T}g(t)=0,$ and\n\n- the lowest point is at $(4,-2),$ so ${min}_{t\\in T}g(t)=-2$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{t\\in T}g(t)=4.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549988 1068 17592186549898 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549988 1069 "q-277045" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549988 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549988 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=6-4\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549988 1072 #uuid "aa587d06-3d76-54dc-bc14-c7a25e414d3a" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549988 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=6-4\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=6-4\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6-4\\sqrt{y} \\\\ 4\\sqrt{y} & =6-x \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{4}(6-x) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{4}(6-x))}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{16}(6-x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{16}(6-x)^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0,\\,f(x)\\le 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\le 6,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 0.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549989 1068 17592186549829 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549989 1069 "q-2765" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549989 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549989 1071 "Factor the expression $4{r}^{2}+16r+16.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549989 1072 #uuid "af2a1028-5c68-5261-af13-01fa26bc76aa" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549989 1074 "Notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $4.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{r}^{2}+16r+16 & = \\\\ 4({r}^{2}+4r+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2}+4r+4 & = \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ {r}^{2}+2\\cdot 2r+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (r+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({r}^{2}+4r+4) & = \\\\ 4(r+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549990 1068 17592186549845 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549990 1069 "q-22308" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549990 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549990 1071 "If $\\frac{{y}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{13}}{{y}^{12}}={y}^{n},$ then $n=$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549990 1072 #uuid "af8fd3b0-1f7d-5ae6-bdf4-ff12a7c85e49" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549990 1074 "We start by using the product rule in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{13}}{{y}^{12}} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2+13}}{{y}^{12}} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{15}}{{y}^{12}} & ={y}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{15}}{{y}^{12}} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ {y}^{15+(-12)} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ {y}^{3} & ={y}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base ($y$), we can equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3} & ={y}^{n} \\\\ 3 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $n=3.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549991 1068 17592186549847 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549991 1069 "q-269387" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549991 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549991 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549991 1072 #uuid "b06d01a6-0f9f-5c10-83f5-9d9f497a0fba" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549991 1074 "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549992 1068 17592186549799 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549992 1069 "q-109828" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549992 1070 17592186045486 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549992 1071 "Given that the $5$th term of a geometric sequence is $-125$, and the common ratio is $5,$ find the formula for the $n$th term." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549992 1072 #uuid "b5f78cff-3c2d-5ff5-b715-c8fb25e53460" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549992 1074 "The common ratio is $r=5,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=-125,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{5-1} \\\\ -125 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{4} \\\\ -{5}^{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{5}^{n-1}}{5} \\\\  & =-{5}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549993 1068 17592186549888 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549993 1069 "q-341802" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549993 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549993 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-3x+3$ for $x\\in [-2,2).$ The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549993 1072 #uuid "bc08f5e3-6f0e-55df-87af-7552a06bb9df" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549993 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and $f(x)=-3x+3$ for $x\\in [-2,2).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(-3x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+3x]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}+3\\cdot 2)-(-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-2)^{2}+3\\cdot (-2))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-(-12)) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549994 1068 17592186549807 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549994 1069 "q-133941" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549994 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549994 1071 "![](images/21/2130538a141293fda6b1bf07c1f0e8e5f7c347a2c688074689b1c13583a11bd2.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points. Estimate the value of $y$ when $x=20.$ \n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear, and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,23)$ and $(9,27).$*" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549994 1072 #uuid "bf3f9846-0d01-5263-932f-08763c5ce37a" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549994 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/a0/a00942c18663c8e9ea2dd2c5354bbe177f7f8b35a4568c5234b9c92b844f196a.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,23)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(9,27).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27-23}{9-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{8} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(1,23):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+b \\\\ 23 & =0.5(1)+b \\\\ 23 & =0.5+b \\\\ b & =22.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=0.5x+22.5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=20$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+22.5 \\\\  & =0.5(20)+22.5 \\\\  & =10+22.5 \\\\  & =32.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=20$ we have $y≈32.5.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=20$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549995 1068 17592186549866 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549995 1069 "q-68963" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549995 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549995 1071 "${({5}^{3}\\times 3)}^{4}=$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549995 1072 #uuid "c20addaa-2ef3-5691-8ace-1f1d2895fe58" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549995 1074 "First, we apply the power of product rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{({5}^{3}\\times 3)}^{4}={({5}^{3})}^{4}\\times {3}^{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the first factor using the power rule for exponents, and simplify:\n\n$$\n{({5}^{3})}^{4}\\times {3}^{4}={5}^{3\\cdot 4}\\times {3}^{4}={5}^{12}\\times {3}^{4}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549996 1068 17592186549809 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549996 1069 "q-341248" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549996 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549996 1071 "Let $F(x)=3{e}^{x}+x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549996 1072 #uuid "c67b1cfd-40e2-5a99-9d17-b7d5268f4dcf" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549996 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{(-x)}+(-x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549997 1068 17592186549859 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549997 1068 17592186549861 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549997 1068 17592186549876 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549997 1068 17592186549884 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549997 1069 "q-355239" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549997 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549997 1071 "![](images/6d/6d8fc9840369a69316c97a443e055bac2cc7b3972c266462c05b1b89424da524.png)\n\nLet $T=\\{-3,-2,-1,0,1\\}.$ The graph above shows the value of $g(x)$ for each $x\\in T.$ Assuming that all the dots have integer coordinates, compute the following:\n\n${max}_{x\\in T}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in T}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n${min}_{x\\in T}g(x)=$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in T}g(x)=$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549997 1072 #uuid "c6c0cc6c-d71f-5404-a9ca-d9e5fa0bb209" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549997 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the maximum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that maximum output occurs.\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n- ${min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is the minimum output value, while\n\n- $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in S}f(x)$ is an input in $S$ where that minimum output occurs.\n\nFrom the graph, \n\n- the highest point is at $(-1,4),$ so ${max}_{x\\in T}g(x)=4$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{x\\in T}g(x)=-1,$ and\n\n- the lowest point is at $(-2,-1),$ so ${min}_{x\\in T}g(x)=-1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{x\\in T}g(x)=-2.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549998 1068 17592186549862 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549998 1069 "q-90975" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549998 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549998 1071 "What is the range of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+1?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549998 1072 #uuid "c749b226-6df0-583d-9d39-da82636ab3b6" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549998 1074 "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=1>0,$ $b=0,$ and $c=1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{0}{2\\cdot 1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(0,1),$ and the range of the function is $[1,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/0a/0a0bb7cbd5494106b36e85f4b9f2604153d28623883c33f507e1ef36389999c4.png)" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549999 1068 17592186549856 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549999 1069 "q-265843" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549999 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549999 1071 "If the sixth term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{1}{16}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{4},$ then the third term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549999 1072 #uuid "cb4d13f6-05f0-58f1-8216-e5e0e82521cb" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186549999 1074 "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{4},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $6$th term is ${a}_{6}=16,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{6-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{4}^{2}} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{{4}^{5}}{{4}^{2}} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=64,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & =64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{2}} \\\\  & ={4}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third term  is ${a}_{3}=4.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550000 1068 17592186549919 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550000 1069 "q-35169" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550000 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550000 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)$ has an inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$, and the point $(2,-1)$ lies on the graph $y=f(x),$ which point must lie on the graph of $y={f}^{-1}(x)?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550000 1072 #uuid "cce239c3-8163-5489-bdbb-7f61254813de" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550000 1074 "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Therefore, each point $(a,b)$ on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ (the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap).\n\nSince the point $(2,-1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ the point $(-1,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550001 1068 17592186549872 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550001 1069 "q-345131" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550001 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550001 1071 "![](images/34/34df0d1bec2fbee41f1d2b81711e83ed3def50d1a439ad2543e7d55743638741.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t}+\\frac{P}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{Q}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550001 1072 #uuid "d2226eab-aaf4-5587-9e42-6e7839242b16" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550001 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=2\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+2{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-6\\alpha -2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{10},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t}+B{e}^{-t}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-3,$ $Q=-1,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-3+(-1)=-4.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550002 1068 17592186549805 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550002 1069 "q-140569" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550002 1070 17592186045486 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550002 1071 "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}-50{x}_{1}+25{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $25{y}_{1}^{2}$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550002 1072 #uuid "d27201ee-27d4-58dc-b2ff-368cd1c77509" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550002 1074 "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-50{x}_{1}+25{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+16{x}_{2}^{2}-50{x}_{1}+25{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}-50(\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{2}-\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{1})+25(\\frac{4}{5}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{y}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+50{y}_{1}-25{y}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}_{1}^{2}+50{y}_{1}-25{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}+1-1-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}+1)^{2}-1-{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & ={y}_{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+1, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & ={y}_{2}+1 \\\\ {z}_{1}^{2} & ={z}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${z}_{2}$-axis." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550003 1068 17592186549893 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550003 1069 "q-63877" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550003 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550003 1071 "Evaluate $\\sqrt[3]{5y+1}$ at $y=3.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550003 1072 #uuid "d9b9c93d-9a95-57ab-bfc8-b62b1c153ff8" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550003 1074 "We replace $y$ with $3$ in the given expression, and simplify as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{5y+1} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{5\\cdot 3+1} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{15+1} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{16} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{8\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{8}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{2}^{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550004 1068 17592186549871 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550004 1069 "q-66995" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550004 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550004 1071 "Express $S=8+16+24+⋯+80+88$ in sigma notation." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550004 1072 #uuid "dd5db24a-0334-5fd9-a195-a6f3785eda3b" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550004 1074 "Notice that the terms of the corresponding sequence\n\n$$\n8,\\,16,\\,24,\\,…,\\,80,\\,88.\n$$\n\n are multiples of $8,$ and since $88÷8=11,$ there are $11$ terms in total. \n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence has the form\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=8n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=11.$ Indeed: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =8(1)=8 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =8(2)=16 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =8(3)=24 \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮ \\\\ {a}_{10} & =8(10)=80 \\\\ {a}_{11} & =8(11)=88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S=8+16+24+⋯+80+88={\\sum }_{n=1}^{11}8n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550005 1068 17592186549857 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550005 1069 "q-106274" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550005 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550005 1071 "What is the domain of the function $h(x)=8{x}^{2}-4x-1?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550005 1072 #uuid "df827c7e-ce3b-5b89-941a-437f86328b7a" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550005 1074 "A quadratic function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the domain of $h(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550006 1068 17592186549832 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550006 1068 17592186549834 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550006 1069 "q-215510" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550006 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550006 1071 "Written in ascending order, the solutions to $|1-2x|-2=7$ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550006 1072 #uuid "e2cc5506-dc0b-5a5b-8907-9924a3a993c1" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550006 1074 "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|1-2x|-2 & =7 \\\\ |1-2x| & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two cases:\n\n- If $|1-2x|=1-2x,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|1-2x| & =9 \\\\ 1-2x & =9 \\\\ -2x & =8 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|1-2x|=-(1-2x),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|1-2x| & =9 \\\\ -(1-2x) & =9 \\\\ -1+2x & =9 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-4$ and $x=5.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550007 1068 17592186549821 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550007 1069 "q-341873" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550007 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550007 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=2x+7$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\n${a}_{1}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550007 1072 #uuid "e5dc675f-f1b9-58e9-a567-64d8cd60aaaf" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550007 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and $f(x)=2x+7$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(2x+7)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+7\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}(uv\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{3}{\\pi }{[(2x+7)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{3}{\\pi }[13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )]-\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\cdot 0-\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{\\pi }){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{18}{{\\pi }^{2}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )]) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{\\pi }^{2}}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550008 1068 17592186549824 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550008 1069 "q-341253" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550008 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550008 1071 "Let $F(x)=x{e}^{x}$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is the value of $f(-2)?$\n\n$f(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550008 1072 #uuid "e7016461-6868-574f-be42-632e56b55fe8" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550008 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-(-2){e}^{-(-2)}=2{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550009 1068 17592186549879 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550009 1069 "q-208694" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550009 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550009 1071 "If $2mn+m+4=7,$ then $m=${{field-1}}." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550009 1072 #uuid "ea72a94a-bdd5-5d3c-b2b4-c2e3488df948" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550009 1074 "We need to isolate all terms with $m$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we subtract $4$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2mn+m+4 & =7 \\\\ 2mn+m+4-4 & =7-4 \\\\ 2mn+m & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $m$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2mn+m & =3 \\\\ m(2n+1) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides by $(2n+1)$ to isolate $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m(2n+1) & =3 \\\\ \\frac{m(2n+1)}{2n+1} & =\\frac{3}{2n+1} \\\\ \\frac{m\\cancel{(2n+1)}}{\\cancel{2n+1}} & =\\frac{3}{2n+1} \\\\ m & =\\frac{3}{2n+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $2n+1\\ne 0.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550010 1068 17592186549915 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550010 1069 "q-54165" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550010 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550010 1071 "Find a value of $x$ satisfying the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{61}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550010 1072 #uuid "ebda143e-32ac-588d-bb70-63d0142c284b" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550010 1074 "Since the cotangent of an angle equals the tangent of its complement, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡{61}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({90}^{∘}-{61}^{∘})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{29}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x={29}^{∘}$ satisfies the given equation." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550011 1068 17592186549838 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550011 1069 "q-133942" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550011 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550011 1071 "![](images/9a/9aa9f94a8de990bdb8332f2efd5c8e945c9bec4847855eebc3fe7f6a3a02a175.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points. Estimate the value of $y$ when $x=23.$ \n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear, and that the trend line passes through the points $(11,8)$ and $(15,7).$*" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550011 1072 #uuid "f0d9aadf-c429-54b7-bed3-89ab52703eb2" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550011 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/b9/b9dbed9bab9d769f7408006854bd0c159cb980bfa5f4441fe1b4e5753dfbc078.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(11,8)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(15,7).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-8}{15-11} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =-0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.25$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.25x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(11,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.25x+b \\\\ 8 & =-0.25(11)+b \\\\ 8 & =-2.75+b \\\\ b & =10.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.25x+10.75.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=23$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.25x+10.75 \\\\  & =-0.25(23)+10.75 \\\\  & =-5.75+10.75 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=23$ we have $y≈5.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=23$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550012 1068 17592186549811 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550012 1069 "q-117951" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550012 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550012 1071 "![](images/30/304c79983ae3d5cdc007d62aa8e30bac247c1370239a9029a33b21b7fcf2f309.png)\n\nOrder the sides of the triangle $△RST$ above from the shortest to the longest." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550012 1072 #uuid "f2983b81-0133-59cc-8912-96155bb43215" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550012 1074 "First, let's find the missing angle $T.$ Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠R+m∠S+m∠T & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {40}^{∘}+{103}^{∘}+m∠T & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠T & ={180}^{∘}-{143}^{∘} \\\\ m∠T & ={37}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∠T$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{RS}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSince $∠S$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{RT}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nRS<ST<RT\n$$\n\nand therefore, the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{RS},\\overset{―}{ST},\\overset{―}{RT}.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550013 1068 17592186549864 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550013 1069 "q-270776" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550013 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550013 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}-10.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550013 1072 #uuid "f2fd8190-fb82-5675-b05f-057d697af7cd" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550013 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}-10\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{y}-10.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{y}-10 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{y} & =x+10 \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+10) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}(x+10))}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{4}(x+10)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{4}(x+10)^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0,\\,f(x)\\ge -10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge -10,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 0.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550014 1068 17592186549917 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550014 1069 "q-148494" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550014 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550014 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x-1}+3.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550014 1072 #uuid "f36e5ef3-ccb2-5795-aca3-91dfb93e14f0" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550014 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x-1}+3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{y-1}+3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{y-1}+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{y-1} & =x-3 \\\\ \\sqrt{y-1} & =2(x-3) \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y-1})}^{2} & ={(2(x-3))}^{2} \\\\ y-1 & =4(x-3)^{2} \\\\ y & =4(x-3)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=4(x-3)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 1,\\,f(x)\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 3,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 1.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550015 1068 17592186549818 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550015 1069 "q-265738" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550015 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550015 1071 "The first term of a geometric sequence is $32,$ and the common ratio is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$  The $12$th term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550015 1072 #uuid "f438fee9-fd2e-5bc4-8ed6-aceb6e4fcc33" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550015 1074 "The first term is ${a}_{1}=32,$ and the common ratio is $r=-\\frac{1}{2},$ so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $12$th term, we can use this formula with $n=12:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =32\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{12} & =32\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{12-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{11} \\\\  & =-{2}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{2}^{11}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{2}^{6}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is ${a}_{12}=-\\frac{1}{64}.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550016 1068 17592186549848 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550016 1069 "q-133882" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550016 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550016 1071 "![](images/6e/6eb063fffcfd0464cbd8cf79811d9ed2ae1e820a30ec621f50e0cc3b9e150e35.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nThe equation of the trend line is $y=5-0.25x.$ Assuming this trend continues, estimate the value of $y$ when $x=10.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550016 1072 #uuid "f611e328-34e9-5ebd-abcf-dae6da9bf9cb" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550016 1074 "We can estimate the required value by substituting $x=10$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5-0.25x \\\\  & =5-0.25(10) \\\\  & =5-2.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=10,$ we have $y≈2.5.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=10$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550017 1068 17592186549813 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550017 1069 "q-148737" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550017 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550017 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=4\\sqrt[3]{x}-7.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550017 1072 #uuid "f867e6a6-dbb2-59f4-99c3-23f6d9793b91" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550017 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=4\\sqrt[3]{x}-7\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\sqrt[3]{y}-7.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\sqrt[3]{y}-7 \\\\ 4\\sqrt[3]{y} & =x+7 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+7) \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4}(x+7))}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{64}(x+7)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{64}(x+7)^{3}.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550018 1068 17592186549806 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550018 1069 "q-66618" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550018 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550018 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)$ has an inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$, and the point $(-\\frac{3}{2},-3)$ lies on the graph $y=f(x),$ which point must lie on the graph of $y={f}^{-1}(x)?$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550018 1072 #uuid "f87f1803-048f-5856-842f-f5c9814dfee5" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550018 1074 "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Therefore, each point $(a,b)$ on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ (the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap).\n\nSince the point $(-\\frac{3}{2},-3)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ the point $(-3,-\\frac{3}{2})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550019 1068 17592186549916 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550019 1069 "q-277064" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550019 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550019 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{x}}+1.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550019 1072 #uuid "f93430f2-f761-5264-8e7c-28207f8a2873" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550019 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{x}}+1\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{y}}+1.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{y}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{y}} & =x-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{2}{5(x-1)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{2}{5(x-1)})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{25(x-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{25(x-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)>1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>1,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550020 1068 17592186549816 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550020 1068 17592186549820 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550020 1068 17592186549860 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550020 1068 17592186549868 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550020 1068 17592186549913 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550020 1069 "q-341777" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550020 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550020 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in [-L,L)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({{field-1}})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({{field-2}}).\n$$\n\nThe $6$th cosine coefficient of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{6}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $F(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ and $L=5,$ the $6$th cosine coefficient becomes\n\n$$\n{a}_{6}={{field-4}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-5}}.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550020 1072 #uuid "fb364528-cf72-5941-b3b8-fc021ca9ace0" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550020 1074 "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k\\ne 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}=2\\pi .$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $6$th cosine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=5$ and $f(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {\\int }_{-5}^{5}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550021 1068 17592186549858 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550021 1069 "q-42981" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550021 1070 17592186045485 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550021 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|}$, where ${D}_{x}=$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550021 1072 #uuid "fc096f25-da43-5ef0-8dca-9310fea097c1" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550021 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+8y=-28 \\\\ 2x+3y=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\,-28 & 8 \\\\ \\,\\,11 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550022 1068 17592186549803 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550022 1069 "q-44680" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550022 1070 17592186045487 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550022 1071 "Evaluate the polynomial $\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3z}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}$ at $z=2.$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550022 1072 #uuid "fe6f82c7-c73d-5fb8-9cbc-ff242831dba6" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550022 1074 "To evaluate, we substitute $z=2$  into the polynomial and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3z}{4}-\\frac{1}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3(2)}{4}-\\frac{1}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{4}+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} & = \\\\ 1+1 & = \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550023 1058 17592186550082 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550023 1062 #uuid "01e04307-8907-58c2-83c8-12dc65028b26" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550023 1063 17592186101355 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550024 1062 #uuid "04ef9537-6351-559a-927e-e766b414f4ca" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550024 1063 17592186299579 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550025 1058 17592186550030 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550025 1062 #uuid "08378717-3017-5e03-97b0-35ada959e212" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550025 1063 17592186549991 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550026 1062 #uuid "0a6e5921-a726-56cb-bfef-3f735f7f9c34" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550026 1063 17592186549955 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550027 1058 17592186550071 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550027 1062 #uuid "0b8293c3-52d1-5621-abf8-52fde1e15572" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186550027 1063 17592186549982 131941395359